{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792713449,"nonce":"FPT5C9HEOZEehBqgibXJaHEVJgM=","title":"Wake idle language clients on input instead of polling","message":"An open code document keeps the client polling the native transport every 200 ms even when no requests or edits are pending. The native stdin writer also uses recv_timeout(100 ms), causing ten periodic wake-ups per second. Replace the writer timeout with a blocking channel receive and an explicit close sentinel; use the loaded window adapter wake primitive when a native output queue first becomes nonempty. UI flushes drain queued LSP input; timers cover request/lifecycle deadlines or adapters without wake support.\n\nProtocol/cancellation/crash recovery and UI lifecycle tests must continue to pass, including welcome/documentless requests and bulk close. Add a native-window test that receives a delayed server notification while the code tab has no LSP poll timer. This issue is independent of the native menu memory retention recorded under the existing memory issue.","files":null}]}